# Yaw the HAND about the vertical line through its own point: the end effector stays
# where it is and only its orientation changes, so an object held at that point turns
# about ITS OWN axis (a bottle shows its other sides) and the forearm swings aside
# instead of staying in front of the object for a camera mounted over the arm base.
# Solved by MoveIt IK from the arm's last commanded joints and executed as ONE
# joint-space move (collision checking off, like ShiftArm — it moves an already-held
# object in place). Measured 2026-10-19: +-0.5 rad is solvable for a held bottle,
# +-0.9 is not (NO_IK_SOLUTION).
#
# A turn can also tilt the hand (pitch) and move the held point a little (dx/dy/dz), so one
# call is a general "show the held object from over there" pose. Everything is relative to
# the pose the sequence started from, never cumulative.
#
# all of yaw, pitch, dx, dy, dz = 0 returns to EXACTLY the joints the first call started from
# and ends the sequence; with no sequence open it is a pure read (no motion), which is how a
# caller learns where the hand is.
float64 yaw             # rad about +z of base_link, through the held point
float64 pitch           # rad about the hand's own lateral axis, through the held point; + swings
                        # the object's far end toward the robot (cap away from the camera, base
                        # toward it), - swings the cap toward the camera
float64 dx              # m, offset of the held point from the start, base_link
float64 dy
float64 dz
float64 move_time       # s; <= 0 scales with the joint travel
---
bool success
string message
geometry_msgs/Point ee_position    # where the hand ended up (base_link)
float64[] joint_positions          # ARM_JOINTS order, as commanded
